1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,886
Every call to a model bills the tokens it actually processes, in and out.

2
00:00:04,886 --> 00:00:09,423
That sounds simple, and the surprising part is what counts as "in" once a

3
00:00:09,423 --> 00:00:11,660
conversation has more than one turn.

4
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:16,693
This is worth learning properly, not just accepting as a line item on an invoice,

5
00:00:16,693 --> 00:00:21,665
because it's the number every finance conversation about this feature eventually

6
00:00:21,665 --> 00:00:22,535
comes back to.

7
00:00:22,535 --> 00:00:27,506
Get the mechanism right here and the rest of this section, cost models, caching,

8
00:00:27,506 --> 00:00:29,619
margins, all rest on solid ground.

9
00:00:29,619 --> 00:00:32,726
Why does turn ten cost so much more than turn one?

10
00:00:32,726 --> 00:00:37,697
Because most chat features resend the entire conversation history with every new

11
00:00:37,697 --> 00:00:42,606
message: the model has no memory between calls, and it only knows what's in the

12
00:00:42,606 --> 00:00:44,222
prompt you send this time.

13
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:46,708
Turn ten doesn't bill turn ten's tokens.

14
00:00:46,708 --> 00:00:50,436
It bills turn ten's tokens plus every turn before it, again.

15
00:00:50,436 --> 00:00:55,532
That single fact explains more surprising invoices than any other mechanic in this

16
00:00:55,532 --> 00:00:56,029
section.

17
00:00:56,029 --> 00:01:01,063
You ship a feature, test it with short three or four turn conversations, price it

18
00:01:01,063 --> 00:01:02,430
from that, and launch.

19
00:01:02,430 --> 00:01:07,463
Your real users have longer conversations than your test users do, almost always,

20
00:01:07,463 --> 00:01:12,310
and the bill scales with conversation length in a way three-turn testing never

21
00:01:12,310 --> 00:01:12,807
reveals.

22
00:01:13,357 --> 00:01:17,520
Read the middle column, because it's the one nobody budgets for.

23
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:21,552
Two hundred tokens of genuinely new content arrives each turn.

24
00:01:21,552 --> 00:01:26,820
Everything else in that row is the same conversation being billed again, in full,

25
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:30,722
because the model needed it in context to answer coherently.

26
00:01:30,722 --> 00:01:36,121
Sum the whole conversation and the total billed across ten turns is eleven thousand

27
00:01:36,121 --> 00:01:40,933
tokens, not the two thousand you'd get multiplying one turn's cost by ten.

28
00:01:40,933 --> 00:01:43,795
That's not a bug in anyone's implementation.

29
00:01:43,795 --> 00:01:49,258
It's the direct, mechanical consequence of 1.2's lesson: the model has no memory, so

30
00:01:49,258 --> 00:01:52,770
the full context has to travel with every single call.

31
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:56,412
Notice the shape of that growth too, not just the total.

32
00:01:56,412 --> 00:02:01,811
It's linear, turn by turn, which means a feature that seems perfectly affordable at

33
00:02:01,811 --> 00:02:06,038
turn three can be paying five times as much per turn by turn ten.

34
00:02:06,038 --> 00:02:07,924
Nobody decided to spend more.

35
00:02:07,924 --> 00:02:13,322
The conversation simply kept going, the way real support and coaching conversations

36
00:02:13,322 --> 00:02:14,103
actually do.

37
00:02:14,653 --> 00:02:19,663
This asymmetry exists because generating a token is more computationally expensive

38
00:02:19,663 --> 00:02:22,718
than reading one, and providers price accordingly.

39
00:02:22,718 --> 00:02:26,750
The practical consequence lands squarely on your feature's design.

40
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:31,821
A feature that asks a model to summarise in three bullet points costs less than one

41
00:02:31,821 --> 00:02:36,587
that asks for a detailed paragraph, even if the input prompt is identical both

42
00:02:36,587 --> 00:02:36,953
times.

43
00:02:36,953 --> 00:02:42,085
Before optimising your prompt to be shorter, check which side of the conversation is

44
00:02:42,085 --> 00:02:43,674
actually driving the bill.

45
00:02:43,674 --> 00:02:48,317
It's often the output length, not the input you spent an afternoon trimming.

46
00:02:48,317 --> 00:02:53,144
This is worth checking specifically because the two fixes look similar from the

47
00:02:53,144 --> 00:02:57,299
outside and cost completely different amounts of engineering effort.

48
00:02:57,299 --> 00:03:02,125
Trimming a system prompt by a hundred words is an afternoon's work for a modest

49
00:03:02,125 --> 00:03:02,553
saving.

50
00:03:02,553 --> 00:03:07,502
Capping response length, or restructuring a prompt so the model naturally answers

51
00:03:07,502 --> 00:03:12,329
more concisely, often moves the bill further for less effort, precisely because

52
00:03:12,329 --> 00:03:15,078
output is the side carrying the higher price.

53
00:03:15,628 --> 00:03:20,601
Say the honest caveat, because the instinct after seeing that table is to treat

54
00:03:20,601 --> 00:03:23,307
resent history as pure waste, and it isn't.

55
00:03:23,307 --> 00:03:28,594
Without the full history in context, the model has no idea what "it" referred to two

56
00:03:28,594 --> 00:03:31,175
turns ago, and the feature stops working.

57
00:03:31,175 --> 00:03:33,693
The real fix isn't sending less history.

58
00:03:33,693 --> 00:03:38,791
It's paying less to send it, which is exactly what prompt caching exists for, and

59
00:03:38,791 --> 00:03:40,553
that's next lecture but one.

60
00:03:40,553 --> 00:03:45,778
For now, just carry the honest number: a long conversation costs more per turn than

61
00:03:45,778 --> 00:03:48,736
the turn before it, by design, not by accident.

62
00:03:48,736 --> 00:03:53,583
Some teams respond to this table by truncating history aggressively, dropping

63
00:03:53,583 --> 00:03:56,793
everything past the last few turns to control cost.

64
00:03:56,793 --> 00:04:01,576
That trades a real dollar saving for a real quality cost: the feature starts

65
00:04:01,576 --> 00:04:05,542
forgetting things a user said honestly expected it to remember.

66
00:04:05,542 --> 00:04:10,766
Whether that trade is worth it depends entirely on the feature, and it's a decision

67
00:04:10,766 --> 00:04:16,053
worth making deliberately rather than discovering by accident once the bill arrives.

68
00:04:16,603 --> 00:04:21,728
That's the definition worth keeping, and the word doing the real work is combined.

69
00:04:21,728 --> 00:04:25,541
Input, output, and history, all counted together, every call.

70
00:04:25,541 --> 00:04:30,416
Run the estimate on your own feature's longest realistic conversation, not the

71
00:04:30,416 --> 00:04:33,228
three-turn demo everyone actually tests with.

72
00:04:33,228 --> 00:04:38,353
The gap between that number and what you assumed is usually the first real finding

73
00:04:38,353 --> 00:04:39,728
this section produces.

74
00:04:39,728 --> 00:04:44,541
It's worth doing this before the model selection conversation too, not after.

75
00:04:44,541 --> 00:04:49,791
A cheaper model looks like the obvious fix for a high bill, but if the actual driver

76
00:04:49,791 --> 00:04:54,228
is history resend or output length, switching models barely touches it.

77
00:04:54,228 --> 00:04:59,416
Diagnosing which mechanic is actually responsible comes first; the fix follows from

78
00:04:59,416 --> 00:05:01,291
that, not the other way round.

79
00:05:01,841 --> 00:05:03,683
One thing before you move on.

80
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:08,764
Do the estimate honestly, using a conversation length a real user would actually

81
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:12,320
reach, not the tidy few-turn exchange in a product demo.

82
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:16,322
Then check which side of the bill is actually doing the damage.

83
00:05:16,322 --> 00:05:18,545
Most people assume it's the prompt.
